    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In order to understand the fracture behavior of folded brittle layers in an anticline, the relationships between joint geometry (spacing, orientation, and length) and curvature were determined through plotting experiment data. These relationships are calibrated using data from outcrops. The fitting equations could be used to calculate fracture spacing, length distributions, spatial density, and orientation. A comparison between Gaussian curvature and the method described here shows that the composite plate method compensates for some deficiencies of Gaussian curvature. At every point of a fold, there are two principal stresses caused by curvature located on the top and bottom surfaces. Both maximum principal tensile stresses are tensile and can initiate two set fractures which are perpendicular to each other. These fracture may cut through the unit under large curvature. The density, and orientation of the two set fractures are predicted using the curvature method. The curvature analysis is applied to Oil Mountain. The results are helpful for understanding the mechanical origin of natural fractures (joints) observed from outcrop stations which are associated with folding of sedimentary rock.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
